1 SYMBOL
    A symbol assignment gives a name to a string, as DCL's do.  When the first
    word of a command is a symbol, its value replaces the word before the
    command is read.  A value beginning with "$" makes the symbol a foreign
    command (see HELP FOREIGN); any other value is the start of a command, so
    a symbol can abbreviate or rename one.

    Syntax:

        symbol-name :=  string            symbol-name =  expression
        symbol-name :== string            symbol-name == expression

    The string is the rest of the line: outside quotes, letters are made
    uppercase, runs of blanks become one, and a "!" begins a comment; quotes
    keep text as written, and are removed ("" inside them is a quote).  The
    expression is DCL's: integers, quoted strings, symbols, and lexical
    functions, with arithmetic, string, comparison, and logical operators
    (see HELP SYMBOL EXPRESSIONS).  Its value, an integer or a string, is
    the symbol's.  To use a symbol's value elsewhere in a command, put it
    between apostrophes (see HELP SYMBOL SUBSTITUTION).

    An "*" in the name says how short an abbreviation still means the symbol:
    after "FO*RTH :== $FORTH", FO, FOR, FORT, and FORTH all do.

    ":=" and "=" make a local symbol, ":==" and "==" a global one.  Local
    symbols belong to the current command level: the terminal's, or the
    command procedure's (see HELP @), and go when it ends.  A local symbol
    is found before a global one of the same name.  SHOW SYMBOL displays
    them, and DELETE/SYMBOL deletes them.  These are DCL symbols, not the
    machine's symbol table that ASM and the debugger use (see the
    debugger's HELP SHOW SYMBOL).

    Examples:

        SD :== SET DEFAULT
        FORTH :== $DUA0:[TOOLS]FORTH.EXE
        GREETING == "Hello, ""World"""
        TOTAL = COUNT + 1
        LEN = F$LENGTH(GREETING)
2 EXPRESSIONS
    The value of an "=" or "==" assignment is an expression, as in DCL.  Its
    operands are:

        integers       13, -5, %X1F (hexadecimal), %O17 (octal), %D10
        strings        "in quotes", with "" for a quote inside
        symbols        a name, replaced by the symbol's value
        functions      F$LENGTH(...) and the other lexical functions
                       (see HELP LEXICAL)

    The operators, from the first done to the last; parentheses group:

        + -            unary plus and minus
        * /            multiplication and division (integers; no fraction)
        + -            addition and subtraction; on two strings,
                       concatenation and removing the first occurrence
        .EQ. .NE. .LT. .LE. .GT. .GE.         compare numbers
        .EQS. .NES. .LTS. .LES. .GTS. .GES.   compare strings
        .NOT.          inverts every bit
        .AND.          bitwise and
        .OR.           bitwise or

    A comparison is 1 when true and 0 when false.  Where an integer is
    needed, a string that holds a number is that number, one beginning with
    T or Y (either case) is 1, and any other is 0.  Where a string is needed,
    an integer is its decimal digits.  Integers are 32 bits and wrap around.
    In a test, odd is true and even false.

    Examples:

        $ BALANCE = 142 * 14
        $ WEEKEND = "Saturday, " + "Sunday"
        $ BIG = COUNT .GT. 10 .AND. NAME .EQS. "TOTAL"
2 STATUS
    Each command ends with a status, a VMS condition value, which DCL keeps
    in the global symbol $STATUS, and its severity in $SEVERITY:

        $ SHOW SYMBOL $STATUS
          $STATUS == "%X00030001"

    The low three bits are the severity: 0 a warning, 1 success, 2 an
    error, 3 informational, 4 fatal.  So $STATUS is odd (true) after a
    command that succeeded:

        $ IF $STATUS THEN PRINT "it worked"

    Bit 28 set means the status's message has been shown already, so it
    isn't shown again.  A command that succeeds gives %X00030001; one that
    fails gives VMS's condition value for its message, where VMS has one.
    RUN gives the image's exit status (showing its message if it failed),
    and @ the procedure's (see HELP EXIT).  SHOW SYMBOL, IF, and CONTINUE
    leave $STATUS as it was when they succeed.  In a command procedure, the
    status decides whether the procedure goes on (see HELP ON).
2 SUBSTITUTION
    A symbol's value takes the place of its name in a command when the name
    is between apostrophes:

        $ TYPE 'FILE'
        $ @NEXT 'P1' 'P2'

    Inside quotes, the name takes two apostrophes before it and one after:
    "Creating ''NAME'.DAT".  A lexical function can be between apostrophes
    too: 'F$LENGTH(X)'.  Outside quotes, the closing apostrophe may be left
    out at the end of a word ('NAME), and a value that itself has a symbol
    between apostrophes is replaced in turn.  An undefined symbol is
    replaced by nothing.  This happens before anything else reads the
    command, so the value can be any part of it, the verb included.  The
    value is then made uppercase with the rest of the command, unless it's
    inside quotes.

    &NAME is also replaced by the symbol's value, later: after the command's
    first word is looked up as a symbol, and after the command is made
    uppercase, so the value keeps its case, and it stays one parameter even
    with blanks in it.  It must follow a blank or a special character, and
    does nothing inside quotes, in a ":=" assignment, or in an @ command's
    parameters.

        $ COUNT = 1
        $ TYPE &P'COUNT'          ! 'COUNT' first, giving &P1

    Data lines in a procedure, and debugger commands, are not substituted.
